#Problem APEX 3.1.13

## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Applications of differentiation)
## DBsection(Global extrema)
## KEYWORDS('derivative', 'maximum','minimum','relative', 'absolute')
## Institution(Valdosta State University)
## Author(S. V. Ault)
## MLT(calculus_singlevariable_absolut_extrema_graphs)
## Level(2)
## TitleText1('APEX Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Hartman')
## EditionText1('3.0')
## Section1('3.1')
## Problem1('13')
## COMMENT('Missing Alt Text');

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl',
    'plots.pl',      'parserCheckboxList.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context("Numeric");

$plot = Plot(
    xmin            => -1,
    xmax            =>  1,
    xtick_delta     =>  1,
    xminor          =>  4,
    ymin            => -1,
    ymax            =>  1,
    ytick_delta     =>  1,
    yminor          =>  4,
    rounded_corners => 1
);

$a = random(-0.5, -0.2, 0.1);
$b = random( 3,    7,   2);
$m = random( 0.2,  0.5, 0.1);

$f1  = Compute("x^{$b} + $m");
$df1 = $f1->D('x');
$f2  = Compute("-x + $m");

($x1, $y1) = ($a, $f1->eval(x => $a));
($x2, $y2) = (0, $f1->eval(x => 0));
($x3, $y3) = ($m, $f2->eval(x => $m));

$plot->add_function(
    [ $f1, 'x', -1, 0, color => 'blue', width => 1.5 ],
    [ $f2, 'x',  0, 1, color => 'blue', width => 1.5 ]
);
$plot->add_point([ $x1, $y1 ], [ $x2, $y2 ], [ $x3, $y3 ]);
$plot->add_label(
    [
        $x1, $y1, 'A',
        v_align => 'bottom',
        h_align => 'right',
        color   => 'red'
    ],
    [
        $x2, $y2, 'B',
        v_align => 'bottom',
        h_align => 'left',
        color   => 'red'
    ],
    [ $x3, $y3, 'C', v_align => 'top', h_align => 'right', color => 'red' ]
);

$alttext =
    'A graph starting on the left edge in Quadrant III, rising to point A '
    . "at x=$a, rising to a point B, at x=0, then falling to point C at x=$m, an "
    . 'x-intercept.';

$ans1 = $df1->eval(x => $a);
$ans2 = "DNE";
$ans3 = Compute("-1");

$options = [
    [
        "Absolute maximum",
        "Relative maximum",
        "Absolute minimum",
        "Relative minimum"
    ],
    "None of the above"
];

$mc1 = CheckboxList($options, [4]);
$mc2 = CheckboxList($options, [ 0, 1 ]);
$mc3 = CheckboxList($options, [4]);

BEGIN_PGML
The graph of [` f(x) `] is shown below, where
[```
  f(x) = \begin{cases}
    [$f1] & x \leq 0 \\
    [$f2] & x > 0.
    \end{cases}
```]
>> [! [$alttext]!]{$plot}{400} <<

Evaluate [`f'(x)`] at the points indicated in the graph and
identify the points as being an absolute maximum or
minimum, a relative maximum or minimum, or none of the above.  
(Select all that apply.)

a. At point [`A = ([$x1], [$y1])`],  [` f'([$x1]) = `] [_]{$ans1}{10}.

b. This means that at point [`A`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc1}

c. At point [`B = ([$x2], [$y2])`],  [` f'([$x2]) = `] [_]{$ans2}{10}.

d. This means that at point [`B`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc2}

e. At point [`C = ([$x3], [$y3])`],  [` f'([$x3]) = `] [_]{$ans3}{10}.

f. This means that at point [`C`] there is a/an 
[_]{$mc3}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
The derivative is
[```
  f'(x) = \begin{cases}
    [$b] x^{[$b1]} & x < 0\\
    1 & x > 0. \\
    \end{cases}
```]
Now since
[```
  \lim_{x \to 0^-} ([$b] x^{[$b1]}) = 0 \neq
  \lim_{x \to 0^+} (-1) = -1,
```]
the function [`f`] is not differentiable at [`x = 0`].
This still indicates that a relative
extremum could occur at [`x = 0`].  According to the graph, there is a
relative and absolute maximum at this point.

There are no extrema at points [`A`] or [`C`] since the
derivative values are nonzero there:
[```
  f'([$x1]) = [$b]([$x1])^{[$b1]} = [$ans1]
```]
and
[```
  f'([$x3]) = [$ans3].
```]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();

